{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Patchset 3 pushed (change 2600), rebased onto origin/main 6125c5345f01 — merge-base is now main's head and rev-list HEAD..origin/main is 0, so the Gerrit merge conflict is cleared. One conflict during the rebase, in the Makefile .PHONY line: main added 'scanner-integration' while this change added 'e2e-deps'; resolved by keeping both.\n\nBlocking finding taken, and taken as a DESIGN rule rather than a second patch. This change's CI wiring has now risked breaking other people's builds twice — first the unguarded 'make e2e-deps' that test_predates_gate_guard_parity caught, now its intolerance of a transient npm failure. Both have the same shape: pre-provisioning is an OPTIMISATION, so its failure must degrade to the in-fixture path, never fail the step. The step now has three explicit skip paths (macOS lane, no e2e-deps rule on this tree, provisioning failed), each emitting a ::notice:: so a silent skip cannot read as success. The Makefile target stays STRICT — a developer running it wants to be told the install failed — and CI tolerates its failure instead of the target pretending to succeed.\n\nAdvisories taken: (a) the collection hook could leak a non-ToolchainProvisioningError — an OSError from the lock mkdir/open/flock would have aborted COLLECTION itself, taking down tests unrelated to the e2e tier; every failure is now converted to the named error kind. (b) the node/npm guard on 'make e2e-deps' had no test; it now has one that runs the target with a PATH containing only 'make' and asserts exit 0 plus the skip message.\n\nAdvisories declined, with reasons: pinning Node to an exact patch version (a yanked patch release then breaks CI, and the lockfile — not the Node patch level — determines what is built); making 'make e2e-deps' omit the browser stack (a pre-pytest step does not yet know the selection, and CI runs the browser tier); dropping the lockfile hash from restore-keys (that is what a restore-key prefix is FOR, and ~/.npm is content-addressed, so a superset cache is beneficial rather than unsafe).\n\nThree new mutation checks, all killed: CI invoking 'make e2e-deps' unguarded; a lock failure escaping as a raw OSError; the Makefile losing its node guard. Eleven mutations killed across the change now.\n\nSeparately: the scanner-integration Verified failure on patchset 2 is NOT this ticket. 'python: can't open file .../scripts/install_review_scanners.py' — that file arrived in 6125c5345f01 and .github/workflows/_scanner-integration.yml is absent from GUARDED_WORKFLOWS in tests/unit/test_predates_gate_guard_parity.py, so the gate that exists to catch exactly this class does not cover the new workflow. Tracked as 90c7-c465-4037-42d5; not touched here."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788499116866580001,"uuid":"23f0e14f-23a1-49c6-aa67-1e054cc624e4"}